#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
HotSpot Bluetooth DTMF Service

BLE GATT server that accepts DTMF commands over a writable characteristic
and forwards them to SVXLink via /usr/sbin/hotspot_dtmf (which pipes the
value into `nc -N 127.0.0.1 10000`).

Protocol for the mobile app (iOS CoreBluetooth / Android BluetoothGatt):

  Advertised service UUID : 6b1d6a10-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
  DTMF write characteristic : 6b1d6a11-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
      - properties: write, write-without-response
      - payload:    ASCII DTMF string, e.g. "91" or "D#"
      - allowed:    0-9, A-D, *, #
      - max length: 64 bytes
  Status characteristic     : 6b1d6a12-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
      - properties: notify
      - payload:    UTF-8 status string ("ok ..." / "err ...")
  Command characteristic    : 6b1d6a13-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
      - properties: write, write-without-response
      - payload:    one of a fixed set of ASCII commands:
                      reboot, poweroff,
                      svxlink-start, svxlink-stop, svxlink-restart,
                      4g-enable, 4g-disable
      - result:     notified on the status characteristic
  Feed characteristic       : 6b1d6a14-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
      - properties: notify
      - payload:    compact JSON snapshot of hotspot volatile state built
                    by tailing /var/log/svxlink: ip, callsign (cs),
                    frequency (fq), talkgroup (tg), current talker (tk),
                    last talker (ltk), tx / rx flags, 4G signal (sg),
                    reflector domain (rf), TX CTCSS (ctx). Fits comfortably
                    under iOS's ~185-byte ATT MTU cap.
  Config characteristic     : 6b1d6a15-c50f-4d86-a7f3-7f2a3a1b2c3d
      - properties: read
      - payload:    JSON with the static-per-session config fields that
                    used to live on the feed but pushed it past the iOS
                    MTU: mt (MONITOR_TGS), ct (CTCSS_TO_TG). Clients read
                    this ONCE on connect and merge into their feed model.
                    Values only change on daemon restart (svxlink.conf
                    edit + service restart). Read Blob (offset argument)
                    is honoured so BlueZ transparently fragments the
                    response for whatever MTU is negotiated.

The server advertises as the device hostname and does not require pairing —
the characteristics have no encrypt-* flags, so iOS/macOS (Web Bluetooth)
connect without any prompt. Android's BLE stack, however, eagerly initiates
bonding on first connect regardless of the peripheral's security flags; if
BlueZ has no pairing agent registered it rejects the bond request and
Android falls into a reconnect loop. To handle that, this service also
registers a NoInputNoOutput "Just Works" agent that auto-accepts bonds and
trusts the remote — the first Android connect pairs silently, subsequent
reconnects skip the handshake. Add encrypt-* flags only if you actually
want to *require* bonded clients.
"""

